{
  "image_name": "1e37926d42a2fdeff304583c2692a9f4.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how SGLT2 inhibition affects the kidney and triggers broader whole‑body metabolic changes. On the left, a nephron diagram highlights the proximal convoluted tubule, where SGLT2 normally reabsorbs sodium and glucose. Blocking this transporter appears to increase urinary glucose and sodium delivery downstream, with changes in the collecting duct and medulla involving NaCl, water, and urea handling, including a labeled urea transporter (UT‑A1). Vasopressin is shown as part of this renal response.\n\nThe right side links these kidney effects to systemic adaptations. The pancreas shifts toward higher glucagon, lower insulin, and a higher glucagon‑to‑insulin ratio. The liver responds with increased ketogenesis and osmolyte production. The heart is depicted as using more ketones and less glucose. Adipose tissue shows reduced fat mass, increased browning, and increased lipolysis, while muscle contributes through a glucose–alanine shuttle and BCAA flux. At the top, the brain is labeled as a central regulator of appetite and carbohydrate preference.\n\nOverall, the image presents SGLT2 inhibition not just as a glucose‑lowering kidney mechanism, but as a coordinated metabolic reprogramming across multiple organs.",
